      <workList xml:id="workDesc_4480fdf4">
         <work xml:id="work_95N2004F">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_af3cab96">C2:003</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_595218dc" xml:lang="de">Ja, theurer, Mattheson, dein Neides-werther Fleiß erjagt</title>
            <title type="text_source" xml:id="title_a6d8f32f" xml:lang="de">Der vollkommene Capellmeister </title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_b4ab5bed">
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_6dff9d6f">Johann Mattheson</persName>
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_123614d9">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
               <persName role="dedicatee" xml:id="persName_4db6be90">Ernst Ludwig of Hesse</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_72b8988f">
               <date isodate="1739" xml:id="date_9f1f4f87">1739</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_26f39f9e">
               <p xml:id="idm69">When Scheibe moved to Hamburg in 1736, he began frequenting the musical and intellectual circles which included among others <persName xml:id="idm70" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Mattheson</persName> and <persName xml:id="idm71" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName> and with whom he discussed music theoretical and aesthetic issues. The following year, encouraged by Telemann in particular, Scheibe started on his fortnightly journal <rend xml:id="idm72" fontstyle="italic">Critischer Musicus</rend> (SchW C1:008). In 1738 he wrote a letter in support of some of Mattheson’s ideas concerning musical intervals, a letter which Mattheson included in <rend xml:id="idm73" fontstyle="italic">Kern melodischer Wißenschaft</rend> (SchW C1:009); the treatise was a precursor for <rend xml:id="idm74" fontstyle="italic">Der vollkommene Capellmeister</rend> which followed in spring 1739. It is in this book that Scheibe’s present appraisal of Mattheson appeared, commending the work. However, in his introduction to precisely the same treatise Mattheson took the liberty of criticising Scheibe’s <rend xml:id="idm75" fontstyle="italic">Critischer Musicus</rend>, for as he says: <lb xml:id="idm76"/>'Half-jesting I must meanwhile express my grief to the impartial reader that my poor <rend xml:id="idm77" fontstyle="italic">musikalische Critik</rend> [i.e. Mattheson’s <rend xml:id="idm78" fontstyle="italic">Critica musica</rend>, a journal that appeared during the years 1722–25] alive was already sick twice, and that in the disguise of a ‘Domino’ in order to conceal her fair sex. But the skinny skeleton of my present <rend xml:id="idm79" fontstyle="italic">Vollkommener Capellmeister</rend> arrived already wounded at birth when it appeared in public a whole year ago, or longer – even before it had seen the light of day; it disappeared again immediately. These are strange title-adventures! People did not show that they noticed the first one at all – the Second Coming is just as silent; although I, at a glance and a superficial examination of the so-called <rend xml:id="idm80" fontstyle="italic">Critischer Musicus</rend>, could nearly have sworn that I had produced it myself' (p. 9). <lb xml:id="idm81"/>In the June-1739 issue of <rend xml:id="idm82" fontstyle="italic">Der</rend>
                  <rend xml:id="idm83" fontstyle="italic">Critische Musicus</rend>, Scheibe deals with Mattheson’s critique which he took very personally (SchW C1:008). He argues that he did not copy Mattheson’s ideas though he had read parts of his treatises: he had also studied other authors. Scheibe states, furthermore, that the title of the journal was inspired by <persName xml:id="idm84" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Christoph Gottsched</persName>’s <rend xml:id="idm85" fontstyle="italic">Versuch einer critischen Dichtkunst</rend> (1730). His first response was somewhat subdued, presumably due to Mattheson’s seniority and position in the intellectual circles of Hamburg. He also mentions their disagreement in a long letter to Gottsched in June 1739. In spite of their differences, Mattheson included Scheibe’s autobiography in his <rend xml:id="idm86" fontstyle="italic">Grundlage einer Ehren-Pforte</rend> (1740; SchW C5:001). However, around six years later when Scheibe was Kapellmeister at the Court in Copenhagen, he published the journal in a second edition, <rend xml:id="idm87" fontstyle="italic">Critischer Musikus</rend>, including notes which are a much more direct and passionate response to Mattheson’s critique.</p>
            </history>
